  "country": "BRITISHISLES",
  "notes": "One combined trip for the United Kingdom and Ireland: the union of the two national lists, a bird on both lists counted once at its commonest. The list is hand-picked (manual) at about 195 species, what a visitor on a normal two to three week trip meets: the garden and farmland birds, the estuary waders and winter wildfowl, the seabird colonies of the north and west (Puffin, Gannet, Manx Shearwater, Kittiwake, Guillemot), and the Scottish, East Anglian and southern-heath specialities that make people travel (Capercaillie, Crested Tit, Black-throated Diver, Scottish Crossbill, Dartford Warbler, Cirl Bunting, Stone-curlew, Bearded Tit). eBird models many of these as rare or very rare, so the automatic cut would miss them. Left out: vagrants and rarities, escaped or feral wildfowl and parrots (Ruddy Duck, Bar-headed Goose, Golden and Lady Amherst's Pheasant, African Sacred Ibis), and hard or very local birds such as Dotterel, Long-tailed Duck, Velvet Scoter, Smew and the rarer waders and gulls. Western Barn Owl is missing because the database lists only the American Barn Owl for the United Kingdom, and Red Grouse is covered by Willow Ptarmigan, the species it belongs to in this taxonomy. Scottish Crossbill is the one endemic: it breeds only in the Caledonian pine forest of the Highlands.",
